      "question_id": "U1",
      "answer": "For a hemodynamically stable infant with confirmed ileocolic intussusception and no signs of perforation or peritonitis, the appropriate immediate management is non-surgical reduction via pneumatic (air) enema under fluoroscopic or ultrasound guidance. Pre-enema management includes: (1) fluid resuscitation with an isotonic crystalloid bolus (e.g., 20 mL/kg normal saline) to address dehydration and tachycardia; (2) analgesia as needed (e.g., acetaminophen) to reduce distress; (3) continuous monitoring of vital signs, oxygen saturation, and clinical status. Contraindications to air enema include: signs of peritonitis (rigidity, rebound tenderness), perforation (free air on imaging), hemodynamic instability (shock), or clinical deterioration. In such cases, proceed directly to surgical reduction. Potential complications during/after reduction include perforation (risk <1% with careful technique), peritonitis, and recurrence (5-10% within 24-48 hours). Monitor for signs of perforation (sudden worsening pain, distension, peritonitis) and recurrence (return of colicky pain, vomiting, currant jelly stool). Post-reduction care: admit for observation for 24-48 hours; monitor for passage of normal stool and resolution of symptoms; repeat ultrasound if recurrence suspected; start clear liquids after 4-6 hours if stable, then advance to regular diet as tolerated. Discharge criteria: stable vital signs, tolerating feeds, no pain, normal stool, and no evidence of recurrence. Risk factors for recurrence include age <1 year, prolonged symptoms, and lead points (e.g., Meckel diverticulum, polyp). Educate parents to return if symptoms recur. Escalation to operative intervention is indicated if: enema fails (incomplete reduction after 3 attempts), perforation occurs, or peritoneal signs develop. Adjunctive therapies: antibiotics are not routinely indicated unless perforation or peritonitis is suspected; anti-emetics may be used cautiously but are not first-line. Disposition: all patients require inpatient admission for observation after reduction; outpatient management is not appropriate. Follow-up: schedule a clinic visit within 1 week to ensure no recurrence and discuss risk factors.",
      "rationale": "This comprehensive plan addresses all aspects of management for stable ileocolic intussusception, incorporating evidence-based guidelines and the specific case details (stable vitals, no perforation, confirmed by ultrasound). The added questions from the consensus process are fully covered to ensure safe and effective care.",
