      "answer": "The appropriate immediate management for this hemodynamically stable infant with confirmed ileocolic intussusception includes prompt air enema reduction under radiologic guidance, provided there are no contraindications. Diagnostic confirmation is established via abdominal ultrasound demonstrating the pathognomonic 'target sign' or 'doughnut sign,' representing the concentric layers of the telescoped bowel. Pre-enema management must include intravenous fluid resuscitation to address tachycardia and borderline hypotension (HR 168, BP 80/52), administration of analgesia (e.g., opioids or acetaminophen) during painful episodes, and continuous monitoring of vital signs, hydration status, and abdominal findings. Contraindications to pneumatic (air) enema include signs of peritonitis, hemodynamic instability, bowel perforation, or peritoneal air on imaging—none of which are present here. Criteria for proceeding directly to surgical reduction include any of the above contraindications, failure of radiologic reduction, or suspicion of a pathologic lead point (e.g., Meckel’s diverticulum, polyp). Potential complications during or after reduction include bowel perforation (requiring immediate laparotomy), peritonitis, and recurrence (in 5–10% of cases); thus, post-procedure monitoring for rebound tenderness, guarding, worsening distension, or recurrent vomiting is essential. Post-reduction care involves a period of observation (typically 4–6 hours), gradual reintroduction of oral feeding (starting with clear liquids), and discharge only after tolerance of feeds and clinical stability. Repeat imaging is not routinely indicated unless symptoms recur. Parents should be educated about signs of recurrence (intermittent abdominal pain, vomiting, currant jelly stool) and advised to seek immediate care if these occur. Escalation to operative intervention is required in cases of failed air enema, clinical deterioration, or development of peritoneal signs. Adjunctive therapies include antiemetics (e.g., ondansetron) for persistent vomiting and antibiotics only if perforation occurs or there is concern for ischemic bowel with secondary infection—neither routinely indicated in this stable, pre-perforation case. Disposition involves inpatient admission for observation post-reduction, even if successful, due to recurrence risk; discharge may be considered after 6–12 hours of observation with tolerated feeds and no recurrence, with close outpatient follow-up within 24–48 hours.",
      "rationale": "This infant presents with classic signs of ileocolic intussusception: episodic crying, leg drawing, sausage-shaped mass, Dance sign, and currant jelly stool. Ultrasound confirmation with target sign supports definitive diagnosis. Air enema is first-line treatment in stable patients without peritonitis or perforation. Immediate stabilization with fluids addresses compensated shock from third-spacing and pain-induced tachycardia. Analgesia improves comfort without masking key signs when used judiciously. Close monitoring post-reduction detects complications early. Inpatient observation balances safety and resource use. Parental education reduces delay in recurrence recognition.",
